1. The Mathematics: Jaccard Similarity
When a user "Likes" a quote, we want to find the next quote that feels mathematically "closest"
to it based on shared tags.

We will use the Jaccard Similarity Index.
It measures the similarity between two sets (Set A and Set B).

The similarity score is the number of shared tags (the intersection) divided by the total number
of unique tags across both quotes (the union).

Score = 0.0: They have absolutely no tags in common.
Score = 1.0: They have the exact same tags.

If a user likes a quote with the tag "Philosophy", we shouldn't scan all 500,000 quotes in memory.
We need the database to quickly give us a small candidate pool (e.g., 50 quotes) that share at least
one tag.

There is a classic Kaprekar’s constant scenario for recommendation engines, where the algorithm gets
trapped oscillating between the same local maxima.
--> To fix this, we need to introduce a "Seen History".
We don't want users to see the same quote twice in the same day,
a relational database is too slow for this constant read/write validation.
This is a perfect use case for Redis Sets with a 24-hour Time-To-Live (TTL).